TE-Anubis — Implementation History
===================================
Append-only log of implementation phases as they land. See docs/architecture.md
for the current-state design and .claude plan history for the full phase plan.

2026-09-17 11:02 UTC+7 | 73f5fd8 | Initial commit
  Repo created (README only): "te-Anubis — The Strict PR Quality Gate".

2026-09-17 12:56 UTC+7 | f58e58a | Phases 1-5: CLI MVP
  Provider-agnostic AI code review engine with a CLI entry point.
  - 11-stage sequential review pipeline (repo discovery -> report output).
  - AI Provider Layer: full Anthropic Claude implementation; thin-but-real
    OpenAI/Gemini implementations behind a common interface.
  - Skill Engine: auto-detection + manual override; four initial skills
    (review-wdio-api/apps/web-automation, code-convention).
  - Repository Context Engine: git diff analysis, manifest reading, budgeted
    per-file context assembly.
  - Review Engine: static analysis ingestion, map-reduce AI review,
    rule-based finding validation, dedup, severity classification,
    max_comments noise-reduction cap.
  - GitHub Integration and Fix Engine left as named-but-empty seams.
  114 tests. Verified end-to-end against real fixture repos through the
  built CLI (not just mocks).

2026-09-17 13:07 UTC+7 | 8750dca | Phase 6: GitHub Integration
  Read-only GitHub Action layer on top of the review engine.
  - anubis-review.yml: pull_request-triggered review, inline + summary
    comments. Never pull_request_target, so fork PRs get GitHub's automatic
    read-only-token/no-secrets sandboxing structurally.
  - anubis-command.yml: issue_comment-triggered /anubis review|fix|explain,
    authorized against the commenter's live collaborator permission.
    Checks out Anubis's own trusted source separately from the commenting
    PR's untrusted head content (never npm-installed/executed) to avoid a
    pwn-request vulnerability, since issue_comment doesn't get the same
    automatic fork secret-stripping as pull_request.
  - Idempotent marker-based comment updates; fork PRs that can't get an AI
    review (no secrets) degrade to an informative comment instead of
    crashing; /anubis fix responds that fix mode isn't available yet.
  167 tests.

2026-09-17 13:18 UTC+7 | d44cfaf | Phase 8: Fix Engine
  Rule-based, fail-closed auto-fix on top of the review engine, usable via
  local CLI --fix/--suggest-fixes.
  - safety-classifier.ts: SAFE only for three narrow, verifiable diff
    shapes (whitespace-only, unused-import-removal-only,
    assertion-addition-only); a file-path denylist forces UNSAFE
    regardless of shape; everything unmatched fails closed to
    REVIEW_REQUIRED.
  - patch-validator.ts: independent structural gate — path-traversal /
    symlink-escape rejection, scope enforcement, git apply --check.
  - sandbox-runner.ts: runs the target repo's own configured
    eslint/tsc/test script before any commit.
  - git-commit-engine.ts: apply -> sandbox-check -> commit, revert on any
    failure; never --force, never rewrites history, never pushes itself.
  - GitHub's /anubis fix still deferred — push-to-PR-branch is a
    deliberately separate, later decision.
  Also extended the AI review schema with an optional per-finding `patch`
  field. Added docs/architecture.md, docs/writing-skills.md, and a real
  README.
  203 tests.

2026-09-17 13:38 UTC+7 | 4551b3d | Phase 10: Security audit + fixes
  Formal adversarial security audit (shell injection, path traversal,
  prompt injection, secret handling, GitHub Actions trust boundary, Fix
  Engine classifier/validator, dependency supply chain). Found and fixed:
  - HIGH: any PR commenter could forge Anubis's marker comments (public,
    hardcoded strings) to overwrite the bot's summary or suppress a
    finding — fixed with bot-login filtering on every marker scan
    (github-actions[bot] by default, GET /user deliberately NOT used since
    it rejects the GITHUB_TOKEN App-installation token).
  - HIGH: CLI error output and the --fix printer bypassed secret
    redaction — routed through utils/logger.ts's redact() everywhere.
  - HIGH: changed-file paths passed to eslint without a `--` separator,
    allowing argument injection via a maliciously-named file.
  - MEDIUM: the "whitespace-only" SAFE-shape check compared lines as a
    sorted set, not positionally — a statement-reorder could pass as
    safe. Fixed to positional comparison.
  - MEDIUM: SafetyClassifier and PatchValidator's security denylists had
    drifted apart; consolidated into one shared module. Also closed a
    symlink-mode-diff gap in PatchValidator.
  - MEDIUM: unescaped AI-generated finding text could forge marker syntax
    in GitHub comments via a successful prompt injection; added
    sanitizeAiText() (neutralize + length cap) everywhere AI text reaches
    a comment body.
  - LOW: dropped an unused `checks: write` workflow permission.
  220 tests (17 new/updated regression tests for these fixes). npm audit
  unchanged (1 low, unrelated, Windows-dev-server-only).

2026-09-17 14:19 UTC+7 | d4010eb | Phase 9: Observability completeness pass
  - Added the `--debug` flag documented but not yet built in earlier
    phases: writes raw AI prompt/response pairs to a local
    .anubis-debug/<timestamp>/ directory only (never stdout, never a
    report, never a comment) — the sanctioned escape hatch for inspecting
    exactly what was sent/received without violating the "never leak raw
    prompts" rule everywhere else.
  - Added logRunSummary() (utils/logger.ts): the one place every entry
    point (local CLI, GitHub review, GitHub command) logs a complete
    RunMetrics summary, so "every run emits complete metrics" is true by
    construction rather than each call site remembering every field.
    Wired into both GitHub entry points.
  - Human-readable CLI report now surfaces duration/tokens/cost (was
    previously JSON-only) — closed a real observability gap, not just a
    docs claim.
  - Caught a real bug while wiring this in: .gitignore's `*.log` pattern
    was silently excluding the just-created history.log from git tracking
    — added `!history.log`.
  226 tests (up from 220).

2026-09-17 14:33 UTC+7 | e945e1c | Phase 11: Testing — coverage audit + real CI
  - Added @vitest/coverage-v8 + `npm run test:coverage`. Baseline before
    this pass: 89.6% statements / 77.4% branches. Targeted the
    highest-risk gaps first (Fix Engine, git commit engine, GitHub
    integration), then static analysis / sandbox tool-invocation paths and
    the skill detector's less-common signal types. After: 95.9%
    statements / 84.8% branches / 98.0% lines.
  - New coverage of note: FixEngine's three defensive failure branches
    (apply-fails, sandbox-fails, commit-fails) — previously only the
    happy path and pre-apply rejections were tested. git-commit-engine's
    `pushCommit()` — previously untested (and, per the Phase 10 audit,
    not yet called from anywhere in production code either) — now has a
    real test against an actual bare-repo remote, including a genuine
    non-fast-forward rejection (no mocking). GitHubIntegration's
    `postExplainComment`/`postPlainComment` — previously entirely
    untested. `11-report-output.ts` (the review.max_comments enforcement
    stage) had no dedicated test at all before this pass.
  - Added .github/workflows/ci.yml: runs typecheck/lint/test/build on
    every PR to this repo — there was previously no CI at all for
    TE-Anubis's own code, only the two workflows that run *as* Anubis
    against other repos. Safe to run unmodified on fork PRs: no secrets
    needed, every SDK/API call in the test suite is mocked.
  262 tests (up from 226).

2026-09-17 14:50 UTC+7 | 4550ab3 | Phase 7 explicitly deferred
  Attempted to unblock Phase 7 (Review Quality / Noise Reduction tuning),
  the one item left from the original plan — its own acceptance criteria
  requires dogfooding real AI review output against real PRs, which
  needs a working ANTHROPIC_API_KEY. Checked: none exists anywhere (not
  as a GitHub secret on this repo, not in the local shell). Offered three
  ways to unblock it (export a key locally for a fixture-based dry run,
  configure the GitHub secret + open a real test PR, or defer) — the
  decision was to defer explicitly rather than proceed on a guess.

  Status as of this entry: every other phase from the original 12-phase
  plan (0-6, 8-11) is implemented and tested (262 tests, ~96% statement
  coverage, live-verified CI + a formal security audit with fixes). Phase
  7 is the one open item, blocked on a real ANTHROPIC_API_KEY becoming
  available — revisit then, not before, since tuning noise-reduction
  without real model output would just be guessing.

2026-09-18 10:38 UTC+7 | 8390a65 (PR #1, merged to main) | Provider auto-detection (Gemini first) + real-key bug fixes; first live dogfooding
  A GEMINI_API_KEY turned out to already be set in the shell (user request:
  "add the capability to use gemini key first"). Added provider
  auto-detection instead of a hardcoded default:
  - `ai.provider: auto` (the new schema default) resolves to whichever of
    GEMINI_API_KEY / ANTHROPIC_API_KEY / OPENAI_API_KEY is actually set,
    checked in PROVIDER_AUTO_DETECT_PRIORITY order (Gemini first) —
    core/providers/provider.ts. Resolution happens once in
    cli/config/load-config.ts; core/ never sees "auto", only a concrete
    provider. An explicit config/--provider value always wins; --provider
    auto forces re-detection. Updated both GitHub workflows to pass
    through all three provider secrets.
  - This is also the project's first genuine live dogfooding data (not
    mocked): running `anubis review` for real against fixture repos with
    the actual Gemini API surfaced two real bugs immediately —
    1. DEFAULT_MODEL 'gemini-1.5-pro' is fully retired (404). Switched to
       'gemini-flash-latest' (a Google-maintained alias, chosen so this
       doesn't recur) and, since the same 'claude-sonnet-4-5' pattern was
       clearly stale too, updated Anthropic's default to 'claude-sonnet-5'.
       pricing.ts keeps the old entries for anyone still pinning them
       explicitly via ai.model.
    2. @google/genai does not retry transient errors by default (a bare
       503 "high demand" surfaced as an immediate single-attempt
       failure) — unlike the Anthropic SDK's own maxRetries. Set
       httpOptions.retryOptions on the Gemini client; confirmed live
       (3/3 successful attempts after the fix, vs. intermittent 503s
       before it).
  - End-to-end live verification (real Gemini API, real fixture repos):
    correctly withheld a finding on a genuinely ambiguous case (a step-
    definition helper returning an unasserted response — reasonable
    given related-file context couldn't show whether the caller
    asserts on it), and correctly flagged an unambiguous one (a fixed
    `browser.pause(5000)` in a WDIO page object) with accurate severity,
    line number, and a working suggested fix, for ~$0.0003.
  10 new/updated tests (272 total): provider auto-detection priority and
  error-when-none-set, the two real-key bugs (default model, retry
  config), plus test-isolation fixes in two existing test files that had
  been implicitly depending on no provider key being present in the
  ambient shell (a real gap the ambient GEMINI_API_KEY exposed).

  Also switched to a branch -> PR -> merge workflow for this and all
  future changes, per explicit user preference (previously every commit
  went straight to main). This change went through PR #1.

2026-09-18 10:53 UTC+7 | 574c23b (PR #2, merged to main) | First real, live GitHub Action review (production conditions, not a fixture)
  Opening PR #1 triggered anubis-review.yml against itself for the first
  time. It failed initially, correctly and as designed: no provider
  secret had ever been configured on this repo, and for a same-repo
  (non-fork) PR the code deliberately rethrows that error rather than
  swallowing it (the fork-only graceful-degradation path in
  github-review.ts intentionally does not apply here). Not a bug in the
  PR being reviewed — a pre-existing repo-configuration gap.

  Configured GEMINI_API_KEY as a real repo secret (`gh secret set`,
  piped via stdin, never placed in shell history/argv) and re-ran the
  job. It completed successfully end to end on GitHub's real
  infrastructure: checked out the PR, built Anubis, ran the full review
  pipeline with live Gemini calls, ran eslint/tsc validation, and posted
  an actual summary comment as `github-actions[bot]` on PR #1 — 18 files
  analyzed, 0 findings, eslint/tsc both passing, provider correctly
  reported as gemini (gemini-flash-latest). PR #1 was merged with this
  comment intact as a historical record.

  This is the first time any part of TE-Anubis has run in genuinely
  production conditions (real GitHub Actions runner, real secret, real
  API billing, a real PR) rather than a local fixture or a mocked test.
  Phase 7 (noise-reduction tuning) is no longer blocked on "no API key
  exists" — a real, working GEMINI_API_KEY is now configured on this
  repo — though tuning itself still needs iteration over more real PRs
  before drawing conclusions from it, not a one-shot "0 findings on a
  clean PR" result.

2026-09-18 11:03 UTC+7 | 2ff5e4a (PR TBD) | Added docs/local-setup.md
  User asked for a quick guideline on using TE-Anubis against a project
  other than itself. Added docs/local-setup.md covering:
  - CLI usage against any local repo via --repo (works today, no
    caveats — nothing needs to be installed into the target project).
  - GitHub Actions on another repo: TE-Anubis has no published npm
    package or reusable composite action yet, so this is a documented
    manual pattern (fetch TE-Anubis as a second checkout, build only
    that checkout, never the target project's) rather than a real
    "install" step. Reused the existing ANUBIS_TARGET_REPO mechanism
    (already built for this repo's own anubis-command.yml) for the
    /anubis-comment-command case, with reviewer/target checkout roles
    inverted from how this repo uses it for self-review.
  Linked from README.md. Docs-only change, no code touched.
